AU  - Gunnarsdóttir, Jóhanna; Philibert, Marianne; Gissler, Mika; Källén, Karin; Klungsøyr, Kari; Loghi, Marzia; Macfarlane, Alison; Sakkeus, Luule; Tica, Vlad; Zeitlin, Jennifer
TI  - Timing of Term Births and Associated Mortality Risks: Ecological Analysis Across 28 European Countries.
PY  - 2025
AB  - <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To explore term mortality rates in relation to rates of early-term birth (gestational ages 37 + 0 to 38 + 6 weeks), regarded as a proxy indicator of practices of elective birth by induction or caesarean.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Ecological study using national birth data.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">28 European countries.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="POPULATION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Births ≥ 37 weeks between 2015 and 2020.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Aggregated data on live and stillbirths by completed week of gestation was compiled from routine sources in the Euro-Peristat network. Countries were divided into three groups based on their percentages of early-term births using terciles (high, medium and low) and mortality rates were compared between groups with random-effects meta-analysis of proportions.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Stillbirths (antepartum or intrapartum fetal death) and perinatal death (stillbirth or early neonatal death) per 1000 total births ≥ 37 weeks.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Early-term birth rates ranged from 17.8% (Iceland) to 49.1% (Cyprus), with terciles being &lt; 21%, 21%-27%, and &gt; 27%. Post-term birth rates were low in countries with higher early-term birth rates. The pooled stillbirth rate ≥ 37 weeks was 1.28 per 1000 total births (95% CI: 1.13-1.46) in the lowest tercile and 1.05 (95% CI: 0.95-1.16) in the highest (p = 0.05), but prediction intervals were wide reflecting heterogeneity within groups. No evidence of difference was seen between perinatal mortality rates by tercile (p = 0.71).</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">On average, the stillbirth rate was lower in countries where early-term birth rates were highest, but no difference was found in perinatal mortality rates. Heterogeneity was high within groups.</AbstractText>
          <CopyrightInformation>© 2025 The Author(s). BJOG: An International Journal of Obstetrics and Gynaecology published by John Wiley &amp; Sons Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
