AU  - Adedeji, Modupe Olatokunbo
TI  - CERVICAL RIPENING AND LABOUR INDUCTION IN POST-DATED PREGNANCY: CERVICO-VAGINAL FETAL FIBRONECTIN AS A PREDICTOR OF CERVICAL RIPENING TIME AND DURATION OF LABOUR
PY  - 2014
AB  - BACKGROUND: Induction of labour is one of the common obstetric interventions and post-dated pregnancy is the commonest indication for this procedure. The favourability of the cervix before initiation of this process increases the chances for vaginal delivery. Over the years the Bishop scoring system had been used but it is known to be subjective due to inter and intra-observer errors. A more objective and reproducible modality for assessment of the favourability of the cervix for labour induction could possibly predict the rapidity of cervical ripening and probable duration of the whole labour process. Hence, fetal fibronectin, a biochemical marker in cervico-vaginal secretion is the focus of this study as a possible predictive tool for cervical ripening time and duration of labour. • OBJECTIVE: To assess the efficacy of cervico-vaginal fetal fibronectin in predicting pre-induction cervical ripening time and the duration of labour in women with post-dated pregnancy and unfavourable cervix scheduled for induction of labour. • METHODOLOGY: This is a descriptive cross-sectional study involving 152 women with post-dated pregnancy scheduled for cervical ripening with transcervical foley catheter and induction of labour at estimated
 9 gestational age of 41 weeks + 3 days. Pre-cervical ripening cervicovaginal swab was taken for quantitative assessment of Fetal fibronectin with enzyme linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA). The duration of time spent for cervical ripening and induction of labour for each subject was correlated with their cervico-vaginal fetal fibronectin levels. SPSS version 19 was used for statistical analysis. • RESULT: The median values of pre-cervical ripening cervico-vaginal fetal fibronectin level for nulliparous and multiparous women with post-dated pregnancy were 45.35ng/ml and 46.93ng/ml respectively. The nulliparous women (83.3%) who spent less than 12 hours for cervical ripening had significantly low levels (<45.35ng/ml) of pre-cervical ripening cervico-vaginal fetal fibronectin ( P-value - 0.000) and the multiparous women (62.5%) who had low levels (<46.93ng/ml) also spent less 12 hours though not statistically significant (P-value 0.2037). An insignificant association was observed between their pre-cervical ripening cervico-vaginal fetal fibronectin levels and cervical ripening time. There was no association observed between the pre-cervical ripening cervico-vaginal fetal fibronectin levels and the duration of labour in the women. (nulliparous- P value -0.6337; multiparous - P value- 1.000). The whole process of labour induction (cervical ripening
 10 time and duration of labour) eventually showed an insignificant association with the precervical cervico-vaginal fetal fibronectin levels in the women. Fetal fibronectin levels did not predict which labour induction process would last either longer or shorter than 24 hours (nulliparous P-value -0.334; multiparous P- value - 0.726). • CONCLUSION: From this study, pre-cervical ripening quantitative cervico-vaginal fetal fibronectin assessment is not a reliable or efficacious predictive tool for cervical ripening time and duration of labour.
